# rules: swift-credential-in-user-defaults
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-312.swift
# from: CWE-312 (Cleartext Storage of Sensitive Information), CWE-522,
#       swift/lang/storage/sensitive-storage-userdefaults.yaml, MASVS-STORAGE-1
#
# `UserDefaults` is a property list in `Library/Preferences`. Nothing about it
# is encrypted beyond the file protection every file in the container gets: it
# is copied whole into an unencrypted Finder backup, it is readable with a
# `cat` on a jailbroken phone, and an app group's suite is readable by every
# app in the group. It is the right place for which tab was open last and the
# wrong place for anything that would let somebody else be the user.
#
# The keychain is where those go, and the difference is not effort - it is
# hardware-backed, it carries a protection class, and it is one call either
# way.
#
# The rule asks the *key*, not the value. A value is usually a variable and
# says nothing; the key is a string literal the programmer chose and it names
# what is being stored, which is the same reason the Kotlin shared-preferences
# rule reads the key. `@AppStorage` is read too, because SwiftUI spells the
# same store as a property wrapper and a rule that knew only the imperative
# form would miss every modern screen.
#
# The receiver holes are variadic. `UserDefaults(suiteName:)?.set(...)` and
# `self.defaults.set(...)` are both ordinary spellings, and a single hole in
# receiver position reaches neither - it matches only a bare name the grammar
# read as a `user_type`. See GUIDE.md.

[ "$$$D.set($V, forKey: $K)", "$$$D.setValue($V, forKey: $K)",
  "@AppStorage($K) var $N = $V", "@AppStorage($K) var $N: $T = $V" ] as $writes
| "(?i)passw|secret|token|api[_-]?key|credential|private[_-]?key|passphrase"
  + "|session[_-]?id|auth|pin[_-]?code|card[_-]?number" as $namesASecret
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $writes) as $all
| ( $all | of($writes) | where_capture("K"; $namesASecret) | focus("K") )
| finding("swift-credential-in-user-defaults";
    "$K goes into a property list that is copied into every unencrypted "
    + "backup and readable on a jailbroken phone; the keychain is the same "
    + "amount of code")
| report
